  "close_rendering": "And he was pierced from our transgressions, crushed from our iniquities. The chastisement of our peace was upon him, and by his wound there was healing for us.",
  "whats_happening": "A group speaking as we describe what happened to a single figure, and the whole verse turns on the contrast between the two. Every noun of wrongdoing carries an our, and every verb of suffering is attached to a him. The Hebrew joins the two with a preposition that can mean either because of or away from, and that one preposition carries much of the weight of how the verse has been read.",
  "key_words": [
    {
      "surface": "מְחֹלָ֣ל",
      "lemma": "חָלַל",
      "gloss": "pierced",
      "note": "A passive participle. The root covers piercing and being fatally wounded.",
      "strongs": "H2490a"
    },
    {
      "surface": "מִפְּשָׁעֵ֔נוּ",
      "lemma": "פֶּשַׁע",
      "gloss": "from our transgressions",
      "note": "The prefixed מִן can mark cause, source, or separation, and the choice between them changes what the line asserts.",
      "strongs": "H6588"
    },
    {
      "surface": "נִרְפָּא",
      "lemma": "רָפָא",
      "gloss": "it was healed",
      "note": "A Niphal perfect, third person masculine singular, with the healing stated impersonally rather than of a named subject.",
      "strongs": "H7495"
    },
    {
      "surface": "וּבַחֲבֻרָת֖וֹ",
      "lemma": "חַבּוּרָה",
      "gloss": "and by his wound",
      "note": "One written word carrying four morphemes: conjunction, preposition, noun in construct, and a third person suffix.",
      "strongs": "H2250"
    }
  ],
  "translation_note": "English versions differ on the preposition מִן: for our transgressions, because of our transgressions, and from our transgressions are all defensible renderings of the same consonants, and they do not assert the same thing.",
